     1	# The Runtime Is Not Infrastructure: A Replication of SKILL.state
     2	
     3	**Draft.** Every figure comes from a measurement in this repository, with per-step
     4	traces and raw data on the `runs/table1-gemini-3-flash-preview-vertex` branch.
     5	
     6	---
     7	
     8	## 1. What the paper claims, and what we measured
     9	
    10	[SKILL.state](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26263) argues that an agent maintaining
    11	**explicit state** executes long procedures far better than one carrying the whole
    12	conversation, and that the gap widens with the horizon. Its Table 1 shows this with
    13	Gemini-3-Flash in a simulated warehouse: ReAct falls from 0.90 to 0.74 between 10 and
    14	200 steps while SKILL.state falls only from 1.00 to 0.94.
    15	
    16	We reimplemented their environment from the paper's description — SkillExecBench is not
    17	public — and measured three things:
    18	
    19	1. **Whether the ladder reproduces** under their own model and decoding settings (§3).
    20	2. **What each runtime actually costs** when you count money rather than tokens (§4).
    21	3. **What explicit state protects against**, through two probes: one on a limitation the
    22	   paper declares (a state schema only protects what it anticipates) and one revisiting
    23	   its recovery experiment with a retroactive correction (§5).
    24	
    25	The short version: **their thesis holds and their magnitude does not; and once input is
    26	billed with caching, SKILL.state's cost advantage over full history shrinks from 7.5x to
    27	1.4x on Anthropic with a short static prefix** — it stays the cheapest arm, and on Vertex
    28	the advantage barely moves. Explicit state protects against losing track of
    29	the procedure; on a second model it does not protect against **writing the state
    30	wrong** — here a semantic copying error in schema-valid patches, a more specific
    31	mechanism than the structural and formatting errors of the paper's own error taxonomy —
    32	and that is what ends up limiting it.
    33	And the largest effects in the whole experiment come not from any of the compared
    34	methods, but from runtime implementation decisions the paper treats as neutral: one of
    35	them silently disabled one of our four arms in every measurement made before we fixed it
    36	(§6).
    37	
    38	---
    39	
    40	## 2. Environment fidelity, and the differences we declare
    41	
    42	The paper publishes the environment design (Appendix B.1), the generator pseudocode
    43	(Algorithm 2) and the prompts (Appendix A), so the comparison is not blind.
    44	
    45	**What matches**: 500 single-occupancy shelves, the action space
    46	(`Store`/`Ship`/`Move`/`Wait`), item destruction on `Ship`, the metric (valid actions
    47	executed / actionable events) and per-seed determinism.
    48	
    49	**What did not match, and is now closed behind flags** (`--apendice-b`,
    50	`--sin-telemetria`):
    51	
    52	| Difference | What we did |
    53	|---|---|
    54	| No maintenance events, so `Move` was never exercised | Implemented: 11–20 per 50-step episode |
    55	| Invalid actions were applied rather than rejected | Rejection with a local error observation, as in their B.1 |
    56	| A third of our steps were non-actionable telemetry | Removed: their Algorithm 2 has no non-actionable family |
    57	
    58	**Closing all three produces no detectable change.** ReAct at T=200, same output cap:
    59	0.905 ± 0.071 in the original environment (n=15) and 0.913 ± 0.072 in the corrected one
    60	(n=19). Difference +0.009, standard error 0.025 — an interval of roughly −0.040 to
    61	+0.057. This is a failure to detect an effect at this power, **not evidence that there
    62	is none**; effects of several points in either direction remain compatible with it.
    63	
    64	**Differences that remain, declared**:
    65	
    66	- **Our actions carry four fields** (`shelf`, `sku`, `units`, `lot`) against their
    67	  positional two. There is more to get right per action, which cuts against the
    68	  "our environment is easier" hypothesis. The failure adjudication (§6) quantifies it
    69	  at 11 %.
    70	- **Our observations are more verbose.** The match is by **context density**, not
    71	  literal content. At T=200, ReAct's mean prompt is 58,127 tokens against their 48,007
    72	  — **above theirs**.
    73	- **Which empty shelf their ground truth picks in `Store`** is unspecified. We take the
    74	  lowest-numbered free shelf. This is the one difference that requires asking the
    75	  authors.
    76	
    77	---
    78	
    79	## 3. The capability ladder
    80	
    81	Faithful environment, `gemini-3-flash-preview` via Vertex, `temperature=0`, `top_p=1`
    82	(their settings). **Nineteen of the twenty cells are 15 runs** (5 seeds × 3
    83	repetitions). The twentieth — ReAct at T=200 — carries 19 runs, distributed 3/3/7/3/3
    84	across seeds because seed 2 was repeated four extra times while measuring run-to-run
    85	noise (§6); its mean over the balanced 15 is 0.905 ± 0.079 and over all 19, 0.913 ±
    86	0.072. In total, 299 episodes have per-step traces and five are aggregate-only. Their
    87	Table 1 in parentheses.
    88	
    89	> **Stateful was re-measured.** Its runner discarded every nested state patch — 40 of
    90	> 5,775 responses produced an applied patch, 0.7 % — so its original cells measured full
    91	> history plus a state block holding no inventory (§6, item 7). T=50 and T=200 below come from a re-measurement with the parser
    92	> fixed (15 runs each, every trace recording the parser version; on Gemini the fixed
    93	> parser applied 3,748 of 3,750 patches); T=10, 25 and 100, marked †, keep the defective
    94	> measurement. No change was detected: 0.996 → 0.996 and 0.912 → 0.930 (+0.017, Welch
    95	> 95 % interval −0.066 to +0.100). No change was detected, but substantial effects in
