   156	ReAct is flat on Haiku: −0.005 between T=50 and T=200, against −0.023 on Gemini and
   157	−0.140 in the paper over the same interval. The full-history arm the paper shows
   158	collapsing degrades on Haiku less than SKILL.state (−0.041); only Stateful degrades less.
   159	
   160	**SKILL.state fails on Haiku, and the adjudication says where.** We replay the
   161	environment with the actions the model executed and compare, step by step, the inventory
   162	the model believes (SKU, units and lot per shelf) against the real one
   163	(`experiments/adjudicar_estado.py`). A failure is attributed to the state only when the
   164	believed inventory prescribes a **different** action than the real one and the model
   165	executed exactly the action the believed inventory prescribes. "The belief matches
   166	reality" means agreement on every field the model wrote; a field it left unspecified —
   167	a shelf recorded only by its SKU, say — is not counted as a discrepancy. Of the 201
   168	failures at T=200:
   169	
   170	- **167 are caused by the state** in that sense; **17** occur while the belief differed
   171	  from reality but the discrepancy does not explain them (both inventories prescribe the
   172	  same action, or the model followed neither); **17** occur with a correct state.
   173	- **In all 17 episodes where the belief departed from reality, the origin is the same:
   174	  a `Move` executed correctly with a state patch written wrong.** `Move` is the only
   175	  transition that requires copying one shelf's contents into another key of the state,
   176	  and that copy is where it breaks — the model writes the contents of a different shelf,
   177	  the lot of the pallet it stored one step earlier, or the wrong number of units.
   178	- **Only in 7 of those 17 did the error reach a decision, all in seed 2.** In 6 of its
   179	  8 runs the corruption happens at the same step (132), after which the model ships from
   180	  a shelf that does not hold the SKU, cascades through ~25 state-caused failures and does
   181	  not correct its state even when the environment rejects the action. In seed 0 the
   182	  corrupted field is a lot number that no later decision reads: 8 corrupted runs, no
   183	  failure caused by it.
   184	
   185	What SKILL.state removes is the need to **reconstruct** the state from history. What it
   186	introduces is the need to **write** it correctly at every transition, and nothing in the
   187	runtime checks that the write matches what the action did. Gemini's belief never departed
   188	from reality in any of its 75 episodes; Haiku wrote at least one wrong patch in 17 of 24
   189	at T=200, and in 7 of them the error reached a decision. The paper's thesis survives in
   190	direction — SKILL.state is still the best arm on Gemini and within 0.02 of ReAct on
   191	Haiku — but "explicit state does not degrade" is a property of the model as much as of
   192	the runtime.
   193	
   194	**Stateful orders the two models in opposite directions.** At T=200 Stateful is the best
   195	arm on Haiku (0.999, above SKILL.state's 0.958) and, on Gemini, is indistinguishable
   196	from ReAct (0.930 against 0.913) and well below SKILL.state (1.000). The same
   197	adjudication describes both — it describes, it does not separate causes, because
   198	Stateful and SKILL.state also differ in response format, parsing, retries and prompt
   199	construction, not only in carrying the history:
   200	
   201	- **On Haiku, Stateful makes the same kind of write error as SKILL.state, far less
   202	  consequentially, and loses nothing to it.** Its belief departs from reality in 8 of 15
   203	  episodes at T=200 — always a correct action with a miswritten patch, the same failure
   204	  as above — but the wrong state prescribes a different action on only 4 steps (against
   205	  168 for SKILL.state), and on all 4 the model does what reality requires. **None** of
   206	  its 2 failures is caused by the state; both happen with a correct one.
   207	- **On Gemini it is the other way round.** Its belief departs from reality in 11 of 15
   208	  episodes at T=200 (5 from a miswritten patch, 6 from a wrong action the state did not
   209	  reflect), where SKILL.state on the same model never did in 75. Of its 211 failures, 86
   210	  are caused by the state, 48 occur with a discrepancy that does not explain them, and
   211	  **77 happen with a correct state in front of it**.
   212	
   213	A hypothesis for why fixing the parser changed so little, which we have not tested: in
   214	Stateful the model's responses go into the history, and each contains its own
   215	`StateUpdate: {...}`. The transcript therefore carried a state written by the model
   216	whether or not the runtime applied it, so the defective arm measured "state kept only in
   217	the transcript" rather than "no state".
   218	
   219	**Memory on Haiku runs into our summariser cap, and a control bounds — but does not
   220	establish — what that costs it.** The summariser has its own output cap (1,200 tokens, with an instruction
   221	to stay under 400 words). Gemini never reaches it — 0 truncated summaries in 30 episodes.
   222	Haiku overruns it constantly: 194 truncated summaries in the 24 episodes at T=50 and
   223	**2,386 at T=200**, about half of all summaries. A control arm at T=50 with the cap
   224	raised to 4,096 (3 seeds × 3, `--summary-max-tokens`) truncates nothing and scores
   225	**0.889 ± 0.126 (n=9) against 0.809 ± 0.184 (n=24)**: +0.080, Welch 95 % interval
   226	−0.037 to +0.197. The control removes the truncation and raises the observed mean, but
   227	does not establish an effect on the score, and raising the cap also changes how long a
   228	summary may be, not only whether the same summary is cut. Uncut, Memory still stays well
   229	below ReAct (0.978). Haiku's Memory column is therefore read as *our summariser with a cap one
   230	model respects and the other does not*, and we draw no cross-model conclusion from it.
