   254	
   255	## 4. Cost accounting with caching
   256	
   257	The paper compares **tokens**; whoever pays the bill compares **money**. An append-only
   258	transcript caches almost entirely; a mutating state block invalidates the prefix.
   259	
   260	Measured on Claude Haiku 4.5 via Microsoft Foundry (T=50, 3 seeds, caching on,
   261	short procedure):
   262	
   263	| Runtime | Score | Raw tokens | Effective cost | Cache saving |
   264	|---|---|---|---|---|
   265	| ReAct | 1.00 | 826k | **152k** | **82 %** |
   266	| Stateful | 1.00 | 873k | 873k | 0 % |
   267	| Memory | 0.87 | 313k | 313k | 0 % |
   268	| SKILL.state | 1.00 | **109k** | 109k | 0 % |
   269	
   270	**SKILL.state's advantage over ReAct goes from 7.54x in raw input tokens to 1.39x in
   271	effective input**, and the ordering inverts: by tokens SKILL.state < Memory < ReAct <
   272	Stateful; by effective input SKILL.state < **ReAct** < Memory < Stateful.
   273	
   274	Two bounds on that number, both material:
   275	
   276	- **These are input tokens, not a full invoice.** Output is not priced in. Mean output
   277	  per episode is 5,740 tokens for ReAct and 9,674 for SKILL.state — neither zero nor
   278	  equal, and they move the comparison in SKILL.state's disfavour.
   279	- **It depends on the length of the static prefix.** With the long procedure (~4,900
   280	  tokens, above the cacheable minimum), the arms that compress start caching too:
   281	  SKILL.state saves 79 %, Memory 48 %, Stateful 22 %, and SKILL.state's advantage is
   282	  2.51x rather than 1.39x. The conflict below is therefore **conditional on a short
   283	  static prefix**, not a property of compression as such.
   284	
   285	Three consequences:
   286	
   287	1. **With a short static prefix, compressing and caching are in conflict.** Every
   288	   method that compresses rewrites the prefix, and rewriting the prefix costs the
   289	   cache. With a long static prefix the conflict softens: the fixed block caches even
   290	   for the compressing arms.
   291	2. **ReAct and Stateful send nearly the same content and are billed 5.7x apart.**
   292	   ReAct saves 82 %, Stateful 0 %. This contrast does **not** isolate prompt order: the
   293	   two runners also differ in whether the history is marked as a cacheable prefix (only
   294	   ReAct marks it), and the Stateful figure was measured with the defective parser of §6
   295	   (item 7), whose state block was empty and therefore constant. Order, cache marking
   296	   and a mutating block are confounded here. **Isolated, the effect of order is 5.2x.**
   297	   Stateful on Haiku at T=50, 3 seeds × 2 runs per arm, both arms marking the history
   298	   as a cacheable prefix and sending the same content (~880k raw input tokens per
   299	   episode); only the position of the state block changes. State first: 74 % of the
   300	   input is cache *writes* and 22 % reads, an effective 869k per episode, a 1 % saving.
   301	   History first: 91 % reads and 4 % writes, an effective 168k, an 81 % saving. Scores do
   302	   not differ (0.997 and 1.000). One of the six state-first runs never applied a patch —
   303	   it wrote shelf numbers as unquoted keys, which is not JSON — so its state block stayed
   304	   constant and cached like the other arm (160k effective); excluding it, the ratio is
   305	   6.0x. We report 5.2x because it is what the arm as specified cost, and 6.0x as the
   306	   effect among runs whose state actually changed. Their Appendix A.3 template puts the state block first.
   307	3. **It suggests one reading of an anomaly in their table, which we cannot verify.**
   308	   Their totals column sits 2.7x to 3.7x below horizon × mean prompt across all twenty
   309	   cells. Caching is one hypothesis — totals billed, mean prompt raw — but their table
   310	   labels the totals as tokens consumed and gives no cache breakdown; the discrepancy is
   311	   unexplained until the authors say otherwise.
   312	
   313	### The magnitude does not transfer across providers
   314	
   315	Measured on Vertex with `gemini-3-flash-preview`, **within a single grid** (original
   316	environment, output cap 600, reasoning budget 0, n=5 episodes per cell) so that the
   317	comparison is not mixing conditions. The figure is cache-read tokens over total input
   318	tokens:
   319	
   320	| Runtime | T=25 | T=50 | T=100 | T=200 |
   321	|---|---|---|---|---|
   322	| ReAct | 1.1 % | 4.9 % | 7.3 % | **7.5 %** |
   323	| Stateful | 0 % | 2.8 % | 4.7 % | 4.8 % |
   324	| Memory, SKILL.state | 0 % | 0 % | 0 % | 0 % |
   325	
   326	Implicit caching therefore **exists on Vertex and favours exactly the arms whose prefix
   327	is append-only**, growing with the horizon — the same qualitative pattern as Anthropic,
   328	an order of magnitude smaller: an input-cost saving of **6.8 % against 82 %** for ReAct.
   329	Other grids in other conditions give other figures for ReAct; the spread across
   330	conditions is not characterised here.
   331	
   332	> These Vertex figures were first computed counting cached tokens twice: Gemini's
   333	> `promptTokenCount` already includes them, and the client stored them again as cache
   334	> reads (§6, item 12). The corrected figures above move the ratios by about 0.4x at T=50
   335	> and 2x at T=200; the qualitative conclusion does not change.
   336	
   337	Paired within that grid, the cost ratio barely moves:
   338	
   339	| | Raw input | Effective input |
   340	|---|---|---|
   341	| T=50, ReAct / SKILL.state | 7.53x | **7.20x** |
   342	| T=200, ReAct / SKILL.state | 27.12x | **25.28x** |
   343	
   344	A direct probe adds a warning. The archived run (`results/probe_cache_vertex_*.json`)
   345	repeats an identical 13,346-token request twice and issues six calls whose prefix grows
   346	by ~3,300 tokens each: **none of the eight reads a single cached token**, while the grids
   347	of that same growing pattern registered 7 %. Implicit caching is **opportunistic and not
   348	reproducible on demand**. We report the grid figures, which are the ones the experiment
   349	actually incurred. (An earlier probe run, whose output was not archived, read cached
   350	tokens on the repeated request; we do not cite its figures.)
   351	
   352	> An earlier version of this section stated that Vertex has no implicit cache. It was
   353	> wrong: it generalised from the identical-request probe, and the project's own grids
   354	> contradicted it — 503,600 cached tokens in a single ReAct episode at T=200. A clean
   355	> measurement of the wrong thing.
   356	
   357	Explicit caching works (13,343 of 13,346 tokens discounted in the archived run) but does
   358	not fit ReAct's pattern: its prefix grows every step, so an object
   359	cached at step *k* covers a shrinking fraction of the prompt at step *k+n*. We did not
   360	measure whether repeatedly rebuilding it pays off; the claim here is only that the
   361	naive use — one fixed cached block — does not apply.
   362	
   363	Resulting statement: **with a short static prefix, prefix caching helps only the
   364	append-only arms, in both providers, and by an order of magnitude more on Anthropic;
   365	with a long enough immutable prefix it helps the compressing arms too (SKILL.state
   366	saves 79 % and Memory 48 %, above). The shrinking of SKILL.state's advantage measured on
   367	Anthropic does not reproduce on Vertex, where 7.5x in tokens remains 7.2x in effective
   368	input.**
