   371	
   372	## 5. Two probes on what explicit state protects against
   373	
   374	L1 measures a limitation the paper declares without measuring it: that an explicit
   375	state helps only with what its schema anticipated. L2 revisits the paper's recovery
   376	experiment (its Experiment 3, an external change followed by a corrective observation)
   377	with a correction that retroactively invalidates an announced fact.
   378	
   379	### L1: explicit state protects best what was anticipated
   380	
   381	A fact is announced at step `t` and first changes the correct action at step `t+40`.
   382	Both halves of that sentence are now checked rather than assumed. The generator accepts a
   383	scenario only if, with the notice in place and the correct policy, the fact changes the
   384	action for the **first** time exactly 40 steps later (14 of seeds 0–39 qualify; we use
   385	the first three, 0, 3 and 6). And on each episode's **real** trajectory we check that the
   386	scored step still depends on the fact; when the agent's earlier choices have already
   387	changed the world so that it does not, the episode tests nothing and is reported apart,
   388	never as a success. All three models, T=50, 8 repetitions per seed, output cap 8192
   389	(Gemini with reasoning budget 0), Claude via Microsoft Foundry and Gemini via Vertex.
   390	Successes over the episodes where the test materialised, Wilson 95 % intervals:
   391	
   392	| SKILL.state | Haiku 4.5 | Sonnet 5 | Gemini 3 Flash |
   393	|---|---|---|---|
   394	| No dedicated field | 0/24 [0–14 %] | 4/16 [10–49 %] · 4/24 | 0/24 [0–14 %] |
   395	| Free-form `notes` field | 10/24 [24–61 %] | 6/15 [20–64 %] · 6/24 | 1/24 [1–20 %] |
   396	| Schema field naming the fact | **24/24 [86–100 %]** | **16/16 [81–100 %]** · 16/24 | **16/24 [47–82 %]** |
   397	| Reminder attached to the observation | **24/24 [86–100 %]** | **15/15 [80–100 %]** · 15/24 | 0/24 [0–14 %] |
   398	
   399	ReAct has no schema, so the first three conditions send it the identical prompt; pooled
   400	they are 72 replicates of one cell. Its reminder condition is the only one that changes
   401	what it sees:
   402	
   403	| ReAct | Haiku 4.5 | Sonnet 5 | Gemini 3 Flash |
   404	|---|---|---|---|
   405	| No reminder (pooled) | 1/72 [0–7 %] | 21/62 [23–46 %] · 21/72 | 0/72 [0–5 %] |
   406	| Reminder on the observation | **23/24 [80–99 %]** | **18/19 [75–99 %]** · 18/24 | **17/24 [51–85 %]** |
   407	
   408	On Haiku and Gemini every episode materialised. On Sonnet 2–9 of the 24 episodes per
   409	cell did not — its trajectory had already diverged by step `t+40` — so its cells show
   410	two rates: successes over the episodes where the test materialised, and, after the dot,
   411	the episodes that both materialised the test and answered it correctly, over all
   412	attempted episodes (24, or 72 for pooled ReAct). The excluded episodes are not counted as successes, although 28
   413	of them happened to take the right action. **The two rates answer different questions,
   414	and the conditional one flatters.** Whether an episode materialises depends on what the
   415	runtime did before, so conditioning on it changes the population and its mix of
   416	scenarios between arms: Sonnet's SKILL.state with the named field looks perfect at 16/16
   417	but materialises the test and answers it correctly in 16 of 24 attempts, exactly Gemini's
   418	16/24; and with the reminder Sonnet's SKILL.state looks better than its ReAct (15/15
   419	against 18/19) but the order reverses on that joint measure (15/24 against 18/24). We read Sonnet's conditional
   420	cells as retention given that the test happened, not as an overall advantage. Only 1 of
   421	the 576 episodes saw the fact change the correct action before `t+40`.
   422	
   423	What holds across the three models:
   424	
   425	- **Naming the field is what works best.** The named field takes SKILL.state from 0–25 %
   426	  to 67–100 % (conditional rates). A free-form `notes` field helps on the two Claude
   427	  models (Haiku 42 %, Sonnet 40 %) and not on Gemini (4 %), and on every model it stays
   428	  far below the named field. "No dedicated field" does not mean nowhere to store the
   429	  fact: the schema checks only top-level keys, and all four of Sonnet's successes in that
   430	  arm wrote the quarantine into `shelf_contents` itself (`{"9": {"blocked": true}}`) and
   431	  used it forty steps later. What the named field adds is a dedicated place **and** the
   432	  cue that the fact belongs there; these experiments do not separate the two. An
   433	  explicit-state runtime protects best against what its designer already anticipated —
   434	  limitation L1, with a number.
   435	- **A reminder works for the runtime that has nowhere to store anything.** ReAct goes
   436	  from 0–34 % to 71–96 % when the fact travels with the observation.
   437	- **But the reminder does not transfer to the explicit-state runtime on every model.**
   438	  On Gemini, SKILL.state with the reminder scores 0/24 while ReAct with the same reminder
   439	  scores 17/24. We report it and do not explain it.
   440	- **The successes concentrate by scenario, not by trial.** Gemini's SKILL.state with the
   441	  named field scores 0/8, 8/8, 8/8 across seeds 0, 3 and 6, and Sonnet's ReAct without a
   442	  reminder succeeds almost only on seed 6 (its notice arrives at step 0, the first
   443	  message of the history). Binomial intervals over 24 runs understate the uncertainty of
   444	  generalising to new scenarios.
   445	
   446	> **Three versions of this probe.** Version 1 applied the correct action whenever the
   447	> runtime returned none. Version 2 fixed the runner but kept the scenarios, which took the
   448	> latest step at which the quarantined shelf would be the lowest free one and put the
   449	> notice 40 steps before it without checking the steps in between: in two of its three
   450	> seeds (1 and 2) the fact mattered 5 and 8 steps after the notice, so those cells
   451	> measured continuous rather than delayed relevance, and in 59 of 576 episodes the scored
   452	> step did not depend on the fact at all (45 were counted as successes). Version 3, above,
   453	> uses strict scenarios on seeds 0, 3 and 6. Between versions 2 and 3 some cells moved —
   454	> ReAct without a reminder on Haiku from 10/72 to 1/72, SKILL.state with the reminder on
   455	> Gemini from 8/24 to 0/24 — but the scenario sets differ (seeds 0/1/2 against 0/3/6), and
   456	> nine of those ten Haiku successes and all eight Gemini ones were on the removed seed 1.
   457	> The changes are results on different scenarios, not an isolated effect of delay. This probe also **withdrew** an older
   458	> recommendation of the project: with one run per seed, the free-form field had scored
   459	> 60 % on Sonnet and become the practical advice ("leave a hatch in your schema").
   460	
   461	### L2: retroactive invalidation
   462	
   463	An announced fact stops being true later, and the agent has to act on the correction.
   464	In the three scenarios used here (seeds 4, 10 and 6) a correct trajectory has **exactly
   465	one step the correction decides** — a property of these scenarios, not of the generator,
   466	which in other seeds yields none or several; once an
   467	agent misses it, its world diverges and further steps start to disagree with the truth,
   468	so counting them scores the cascade, not the correction. The measure is therefore per
   469	episode: does the agent get the decisive step right? All three models at T=50, seeds 4,
   470	10 and 6 × 8 repetitions, output cap 8192 (Gemini with reasoning budget 0), Claude via
   471	Microsoft Foundry and Gemini via Vertex:
   472	
   473	| Model | ReAct applies the correction | SKILL.state |
   474	|---|---|---|
   475	| Haiku 4.5 | 1/24 | **24/24** |
   476	| Sonnet 5 | 9/24 | **22/24** |
   477	| Gemini 3 Flash | 0/24 | **24/24** |
   478	
   479	**Explicit state applies the correction in 70 of 72 episodes (97 %, Wilson 95 % interval
   480	90–99 %); full history in 10 of 72 (14 %, 8–24 %).** Every episode had its decisive step,
   481	and no response was truncated at it — seven Sonnet/ReAct episodes truncated elsewhere,
   482	and none was excluded. The misses are not other errors: every failed decisive step is
   483	exactly the action of an agent that never heard the correction. The successes
   484	concentrate by scenario rather than by trial — Sonnet with ReAct scores 2/8, 0/8 and
   485	7/8 across the three seeds, and its two SKILL.state misses are both in seed 4 — so the
   486	binomial intervals understate the uncertainty of generalising to new scenarios.
   487	
   488	> **This replaces an earlier measurement that did not measure what it published.** The
   489	> previous version of this section reported "93 of 93" and "132 of 132" dependent steps
   490	> with explicit state. Its scorer took the dependent steps from the trajectory of a
   491	> *simulated* deaf agent, counted successes as dependent steps minus deaf actions without
   492	> deducting steps with no action or with another error, and its runner applied the
   493	> *correct* action whenever the runtime returned none. A runtime that never answers
   494	> scored 100 % (§6, item 11). The direction survives; the perfection does not.
   495	
   496	### Across the three models
   497	
   498	Gemini with ReAct never applies either correction — 0 of 72 in L1 without a reminder, 0
   499	of 24 in L2 — while scoring 0.913 on the long procedure; Haiku scores 0.974 at T=200 and
   500	applies the L2 correction once in 24. **High long-procedure scores coexist with poor
   501	probe performance on Haiku and Gemini, and probe performance differs sharply between
   502	runtimes on all three models.** We did not measure Sonnet's long procedure, and we make
   503	no predictive claim across models. Both probes are measured with the same cap and design on the three models;
   504	their decoding still differs between providers (§3).
   505	
   506	---
   507	
   508	## 6. Instrument and artefacts
   509	
   510	This is the section that separates this from a blog post, and the one that cost us most.
   511	
   512	**Nine artefacts catalogued** in the first block, all with the same signature: an
   513	implicit contract between runtime and model. The runtime assumed something it never
   514	declared, and the result depended on whether the model guessed right. The first three
   515	cost entire grids; **two surfaced in the audit before publication, and both had produced
   516	a written result, with tables and intervals, ready to send**.
   517	
   518	This second block added six more, and they are worth enumerating because they transfer:
   519	
   520	1. **Greedy decoding does not guarantee reproducibility.** The protocol assumed that at
   521	   `temperature=0` seeds are environment instances and one run per cell suffices. The
   522	   same cell repeated five times gives between 0.830 and 0.960. **Three already-written
   523	   conclusions collapsed on repetition**: an environment effect of −5.8 points that
   524	   turned out to be +0.008, a "monotonic" noise curve that turned out flat, and a
   525	   noise estimate of eleven points that turned out to be a tail.
   526	2. **The distribution has a left tail because failures cascade.** Across five runs of one
   527	   cell the sample deviation is 0.052; **excluding the 0.830 outlier** it is 0.012, and
   528	   three runs of the same cell at T=100 give 0.006. We quote both, because the second
   529	   number is conditional on removing the very run that motivates the point. What forces
   530	   repetition is not the ordinary spread: it is the tails.
   531	3. **The runner discarded the model's responses.** It kept the score and the token
   532	   counts, so `correct=False` was an indistinguishable zero: you could not tell whether
   533	   the model lost track of state — what the experiment measures — or miscopied a JSON
   534	   field. With responses retained, adjudicating 45 failures across four traces of one
   535	   cell gives **32 wrong-shelf (71 %), 7 wrong action type (16 %), 5 field-copy errors
   536	   (11 %), 1 unparseable**.
   537	4. **A truncated episode counted as complete.** Aggregation counted files, not steps; an
   538	   episode of 163 steps out of 200 entered the mean. The bias was two thousandths, but
   539	   it was only found because completeness was checked as a matter of routine.
   540	5. **Traces that did not record their own conditions.** For most of this work the traces
   541	   stored no output cap, reasoning budget, provider or responding model ID. Two files
   542	   from the same cell could come from different caps and nothing would say so — which is
   543	   how a −5.8-point "environment effect" survived long enough to be written down. The
   544	   runners now write a conditions header and per-step token usage — but **only runs made
   545	   after that change carry it**. The historical Gemini traces — Table 1 except the
   546	   Stateful re-measurement, the noise table and the environment comparison — do not, so
   547	   their conditions are certified by the filename and the run log, not by the artefact
   548	   itself. Every trace behind the Haiku table, both Stateful re-measurements, the Memory
   549	   control and the prompt-order experiment does.
   550	6. **A probe that measured the wrong pattern.** The cache probe repeated an identical
   551	   request and concluded that Vertex has no implicit cache, while the project's own
   552	   grids recorded 503,600 cached tokens in one episode. The probe was correct; the
   553	   pattern it probed was not the one the experiment uses.
   554	
   555	A third block, found while measuring the second model, contains the most expensive
   556	artefact of the project:
   557	
   558	7. **One of the four arms never ran.** Stateful read its state patch with a non-greedy
   559	   regex that stopped at the first closing brace. Every nested patch — the normal case,
   560	   `{"shelf_contents": {"3": {...}}}` — was truncated, failed to parse, and was dropped
   561	   without a warning: **40 of 5,775 responses on Gemini produced an applied patch, 0 of
   562	   6,000 on Haiku**. What both
   563	   Stateful columns measured is full history plus a state block with no inventory — empty
   564	   on Haiku, and on Gemini holding only the occasional `last_event` (the 40 patches, in
   565	   11 episodes). Its unit test
   566	   used a flat value and passed. It surfaced only because Stateful beat SKILL.state on
   567	   Haiku and we went to the traces to find out why. Re-measured with the parser fixed,
   568	   no change was detected (§3). The first fix was itself incomplete: on Haiku it still
   569	   dropped every patch written in Markdown — 731 of 3,000 at T=200 — which the third
   570	   adversarial review found, not us; a third parser version closed it. A defect that
   571	   changes nothing measurable is still a defect in what the column claims to measure.
   572	8. **A cap one model respects and the other does not.** The summariser's own cap was
   573	   never reached on Gemini and was exceeded in half the summaries on Haiku (§3). A cap
   574	   is not a neutral constant across models; it has to be checked per model, in the
   575	   traces.
   576	9. **Resuming re-paid finished work.** The completeness check counted the conditions
   577	   header as a step, so no episode ever matched its horizon and every relaunch re-ran
   578	   episodes already measured. It was caught on the first relaunch of R2 because a cell
   579	   finished minutes earlier started again.
   580	10. **The traces did not record cache writes.** The client received them; the trace row
   581	    did not store them. In the first run of the prompt-order experiment the arm that
   582	    rewrites its cache every step — the most expensive case — showed ~540 prompt tokens
   583	    per step and looked the cheapest. Those twelve episodes were discarded and re-run.
   584	    The §4 table does not depend on it: it was computed by the grid runner, which counted
   585	    writes.
   586	
   587	11. **A probe that could not fail.** The first L2 scorer counted dependent steps on a
   588	    simulated trajectory and subtracted only deaf actions, and its runner repaired the
   589	    world whenever the runtime returned no action; a runtime that never answers scored
   590	    100 %. It was the section of this draft we had checked least, because its result was
   591	    the cleanest. The fifth adversarial review found it. Re-measured with the decisive
   592	    step read from the real trajectory, explicit state goes from "132 of 132" to 70 of
   593	    72 episodes. L1 had the same world-repairing runner and a second defect of design,
   594	    found by the sixth review: its fact was not delayed in two of its three scenarios.
   595	    It was redesigned and re-measured (§5).
   596	
   597	12. **Two providers, two meanings of "prompt tokens".** Anthropic's `input_tokens`
   598	    excludes cached tokens; Gemini's `promptTokenCount` includes them. The client stored
   599	    both the same way and the cost function added cached tokens on top, so every Vertex
   600	    cost figure counted the cache twice. The eighth adversarial review found it; the §4
   601	    Vertex figures are recomputed.
   602	
   603	Items 5 and 6 were found by an **independent adversarial review of this draft against
   604	the raw traces**, not by us. That review also corrected the failure classification
   605	above, the maintenance-event range in §2, the compression factor in §3 and the sample
   606	size in §3. We recommend the procedure, not the anecdote: have something recompute every
   607	published figure from the artefacts, without access to the prose that justifies it.
   608	
   609	Why traces matter, verbatim from one episode: at step 12 the model stores the SKU of a
   610	different pallet; at step 16 it asserts *"Shelf 0 (SKU-F, stored in step 12)"* when it
   611	had itself stored SKU-B there. **The early error contaminates later decisions** — and
   612	that, not sampling noise, is what produces the tail.
   613	
   614	**The transferable claim**: the paper treats the runtime as neutral infrastructure — a
   615	merge operator, a patch format, a schema, a prompt order. In this project, individual
   616	runtime decisions moved results by amounts comparable to or larger than the difference
   617	between the methods being compared: the schema field decides 0 % versus 67 % on the
   618	dependent step (§5), and a parser that silently dropped state patches decided what one
   619	of four arms measured until it was fixed (§6, item 7). We have not measured every such
   620	decision, so we make no claim about a general range.
   621	
   622	**Repository artefacts**: environment with fidelity flags, per-step traces, completeness
   623	verifier, a density meter that does not call the API, truncation counters, per-episode
   624	checkpointing and end-to-end cache accounting — except in the L1 aggregates, whose cost
   625	fields ignored cache reads and writes until the last re-measurement, and in every Gemini
   626	figure computed before item 12 below, which counted cached tokens twice. The per-step
   627	traces record the raw provider fields and are the source to use.
   628	
   629	---
   630	
   631	## 7. Limitations
   632	
   633	- **`gemini-3-flash-preview` may not be their `Gemini-3-Flash`.** Declared as a risk
   634	  from the outset. Vertex returns the responding model ID on every call and it is
   635	  recorded per step, but not in the traces measured before that change. The Anthropic
   636	  client did not copy it until the last Stateful re-measurement: from then on — its 30
   637	  episodes and the prompt-order experiment — every step records
   638	  `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`; the earlier Haiku traces record only the requested alias
   639	  in their header.
   640	- **A reimplemented environment is not their environment.** We matched what their
   641	  appendix allows us to compare, and the remaining differences are in §2. The shelf
   642	  choice in `Store` still awaits an answer from the authors.
   643	- **Our Memory is not comparable to theirs.** Our summariser compresses fourteen times
   644	  more (5,770-token mean prompt against their 84,364 at T=200).
   645	  What we measured is our summarisation policy, not "summarisation" as a category.
   646	- **We have not proven the environment gaps have no effect.** The measured difference is
   647	  +0.009, with an interval of roughly −0.040 to +0.057 (§2): no change was detected, and
   648	  effects of several points in either direction remain compatible. The same caveat
   649	  applies to the three lowest noise levels in §3.
   650	- **Cell sizes are not uniform.** Nineteen cells carry 15 runs; ReAct at T=200 carries
   651	  19, unbalanced across seeds (3/3/7/3/3). Five of its episodes exist only as
   652	  aggregates, without traces.
   653	- **Cost figures price input only**, and the caching results depend on the length of the
   654	  static prefix (§4).
   655	- **The probes share cap and design across models, not decoding.** Both were re-measured
   656	  on all three models with the corrected runner (§5); Gemini runs with temperature 0 and
   657	  reasoning budget 0, Claude without either setting.
   658	- **One environment.** The second (Software Repository) was retired because it could not
   659	  measure what it was built for, not because the arms scored alike — they did not
   660	  (Haiku: SKILL.state 0.974, ReAct 0.895; Sonnet: 0.876 and 1.000, 9 episodes each). Its
   661	  dependency always sat exactly two steps after the event that created it, so it had no
   662	  delayed relevance to measure (`docs/resultados-bloque1.md`, §4.ter). We do not
   663	  attribute the score differences above to rule or routine steps: a separate diagnostic
   664	  sample of three episodes per arm shows both kinds of failure, and the nine-episode
   665	  cohorts themselves were not adjudicated.
   666	- **SKILL.state's ceiling depends on the model.** At 1.000 with zero variance on Gemini
   667	  the task did not discriminate at the top; on Haiku it does (0.958 at T=200), and the
   668	  failure is in writing the state (§3). Two models are not enough to say which models
   669	  make that error.
   670	- **Stateful has been re-measured only at T=50 and T=200**, with parser version 2 on
   671	  Gemini (which applied 3,748 of 3,750 of its patches) and version 3 on Haiku. The
   672	  intermediate horizons of Gemini's Table 1 keep the defective measurement, marked as
   673	  such.
   674	- **Haiku and Gemini do not share decoding settings** (§3): the cross-model differences
   675	  are between two configured systems, not two models under identical conditions.
   676	- **Haiku's Memory column mixes the summarisation policy with summary truncation** (§3);
   677	  the control arm has n=9 and bounds the effect at T=50 only.

